<p>In his <a href="https://www.google.com/books/edition/Trelawny_s_Recollections_of_the_Last_Day/Ms1oOf3fV5AC?hl=en&#038;gbpv=1&#038;pg=PA41&#038;printsec=frontcover"><em>Recollections of the Last Days of Shelley and Byron</em></a>, Edward John Trelawny describes Shelley&#8217;s thirst for knowledge as &#8220;unquenchable&#8221;:</p>
<blockquote><p>
I called on him one morning at ten, he was in his study with a German folio open, resting on the broad marble mantel-piece, over an old-fashioned fire-place, and with a dictionary in his hand. He always read standing if possible. He had promised over night to go with me, but now begged me to let him off. I then rode to Leghorn, eleven or twelve miles distant, and passed the day there; on returning at six in the evening to dine with Mrs. Shelley and the Williams&#8217;s, as I had engaged to do, I went into the Poet&#8217;s room and found him exactly in the position in which I had left him in the morning, but looking pale and exhausted.
</p></blockquote>
<p>Trelawny pointed to a plate of bread and meat and Shelley admitted that this must have been his dinner; &#8220;I thought I had eaten it.&#8221; Trelawny pulled him into the dining room, &#8220;but he brought a book with him and read more than he ate.&#8221;</p>
